<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mughal karkhanas</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as karkhana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mughal karkhanas were the workshops and storehouses of craftsmen established by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> in their empire to meet the needs of the state, the emperor's household and the army; karkhana is a Hindi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> word meaning factory. Maintained by the state, nobles, mansabdars and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a>, and headed for the emperor by the khan-i-saman, they made arms and armour, textiles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shawl">shawls</a>, carpets, jewellery, perfumes and tents; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ain-i-akbari">Ain-i-Akbari</a> lists 36 classes. Artisans there worked under controllers' orders rather than on their own account, accounts were kept for each workshop, and skilled workers earned more in the imperial karkhanas. They declined with the Mughal Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mughal-karkhanas.jpg" alt="Mughal karkhanas" width="330" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Simpson · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Simpson_-_Thugs_in_the_School_of_Industry,_Jubblepoor_-_B1975.3.263_-_Yale_Center_for_British_Art.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
